{"name":"Clear Brook","hex":"#60949b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"clear","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Transparent in colour.","Bright; luminous; not dark or obscured."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["All the way; entirely.","Not near something or touching it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clear"},{"term":"brook","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To bear; endure; put up with; tolerate.","To enjoy the use of; make use of; profit by; to use, enjoy, possess, or hold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A body of running water smaller than a river; a small stream.","A water meadow, or  low, marshy ground."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brook"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thorhild County","blocks":[{"text":"Thorhild County is a municipal district located in the central part of northern Alberta, Canada, in Census Division 13. It was incorporated in 1955. It changed its name from the County of Thorhild No. 7 to Thorhild County on March 20, 2013."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/AB_locator_THORHILD_COUNTY.svg/330px-AB_locator_THORHILD_COUNTY.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thorhild_County"},{"title":"Clearbrook, Abbotsford","blocks":[{"text":"Clearbrook is a neighbourhood of the City of Abbotsford, British Columbia, located in the western portion of the city in the vicinity of Clearbrook Road. The area was originally known as Sandy Flats or West Abbotsford, and was located in the Poplar district of the District of Matsqui. It became known as Clearbrook by the late 1940s, most likely named after Clearbrook Road, a road that originally ran from Old Yale Road south across the Canada-USA border to nearby Clearbrook, Washington."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clearbrook%2C_Abbotsford"},{"title":"Clear Brook (Nanticoke River tributary)","blocks":[{"text":"Clear Brook is a 23.02 mi (37.05 km) long tributary to the Nanticoke River in Sussex County, Delaware. It joins the Nanticoke at \"Williams Pond\" in the City of Seaford, Delaware."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Clear_Brook_downstream_of_Hearns_Pond.jpg/330px-Clear_Brook_downstream_of_Hearns_Pond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clear_Brook_(Nanticoke_River_tributary)"},{"title":"Transparency and translucency","blocks":[{"text":"In the field of optics, transparency is the physical property of allowing light to pass through the material without appreciable scattering of light. On a macroscopic scale, the photons can be said to follow Snell's law. Translucency is the physical property of allowing light to pass through the material. It allows light to pass through but the light does not necessarily follow Snell's law on the macroscopic scale; the photons may be scattered at either of the two interfaces, or internally, where there is a change in the index of refraction. In other words, a translucent material is made up of components with different indices of refraction, while a transparent material is made up of components with a uniform index of refraction. Transparent materials appear clear, with the overall appearance of one color, or any combination leading up to a brilliant spectrum of every color. The opposite property of translucency is opacity. Other categories of visual appearance, related to the perception of regular or diffuse reflection and transmission of light, have been organized under the concept of cesia in an order system with three variables, including transparency, translucency and opacity among the involved aspects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Dichroic_filters.jpg/330px-Dichroic_filters.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transparency_and_translucency"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Backlit_mushroom.jpg/500px-Backlit_mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Backlit_mushroom.jpg/1280px-Backlit_mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Backlit_mushroom.jpg/500px-Backlit_mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Translucency of a material being used to highlight the structure of a mushroom","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABacklit_mushroom.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif/250px-1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9b/1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif/250px-1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Normal modes of vibration in a crystalline solid","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1D_normal_modes_(280_kB).gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Laser_in_fibre.jpg/500px-Laser_in_fibre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Laser_in_fibre.jpg/1280px-Laser_in_fibre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Laser_in_fibre.jpg/500px-Laser_in_fibre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=